//@ normative
//@ unimplemented "Every import form in the file parses, `import * from m;` included, and this note's claim that none of them resolves is corrected here, 2026-08-27: measured from the repository root, which is where the corpus is compiled, all seven parse. Two of the claims that followed here are corrected, 2026-09-07, and both were true when written. The file now reports four diagnostics and none of them is at 23:20. Line 4's `import { printf } from stdio;` is worth naming separately, because it is the measurement that disproved interfaces.fin's note -- printf is findable through the bundled library today, so a sample that fails on printf fails for want of a binding and not for want of a search path. The working-directory dependence this note recorded is real and still there, and I re-measured it rather than deleting it: run from a different directory, line 3's `import { Vector3 } from \"tests/samples/structs.fin\"` gives `module not found: tests/samples/structs.fin` and takes line 7's `import \"tests/samples/macros.fin\"` down with it. A quoted path is tried relative to the importing file first, then as an absolute path, then through the search paths -- and the search-path set ends in the working directory, which is the step that resolves these two from the repository root and nothing else. So the file's own claim on line 9, that the compiler needs `FIN_LIBS` or `--fin-libs` to know where a pathless library lives, is answered: 14's `import { HashMap } from hashmap::std` reaches `lib/std/hashmap.fin` through exactly that step. Lines 9 and 11 used to reach `lib/std/somelib` and no longer do, and that is a decision rather than a regression: `lib/std/somelib/index.fin` was deleted in `4d79ae7`, \"somelib isnt a real stdlib module\", because the name is this sample's invention and shipping a module to satisfy it would have made the library answer to the corpus instead of the other way round. So 9 and 11 report `module not found: somelib` and 9 takes a `Failed to load module 'somelib'` with it, twice over -- four of this file's four diagnostics. The mechanism the paragraph above describes is unchanged and is still what the sample was written to ask about; what is gone is the module, not the search. `macros.magic_add!` at 23:20 is resolved, and how it was resolved is the part worth keeping. This note used to book it as unrepairable and it was right about the options it had: deleting line 23 would remove the construct the file exists to specify, and declaring `magic_add!` would write a macro the corpus did not prove -- tests/samples/macro_definitions.fin, the only other macro-definition text in the corpus, is entirely inside a `/* [WIP] */` block and declares nothing. What changed is not the sample's evidence but the language's: ADR 0023 settled the macro form on the corpus's own terms, and its step 8 wrote `@macro magic_add(a, b) { return quote { $a + $b; }; }` into tests/samples/macros.fin as one of that step's four declarations. So the macro is no longer invented -- it is specified, by an ADR that read this line as one of its inputs -- and line 7's comment about macros arriving \"as `macros` by default so it can be used like `macros.magic_add!(...)`\" is now a true statement about the compiler rather than a wish. The parenthesised bracket keeps each argument an expression (ADR 0023 step 3), so 23 expands to `10 + 20` and `<auto>` infers `int`. Both corrections replaced text inside this note line and changed no other line, so the file is 24 lines as it was"
import { Vector3 } from "tests/samples/structs.fin"; // This is allowed and ONLY imports the symbol (Vector3) [supports special functions, macros, ...]
import { printf } from stdio; // this looks in builtins directory which we haven't implemented yet but it looks for either a file named "stdio.fin" or "stdio.finlib" OR looks for a directory named "stdio" and looks for a file named "printf.fin" in it (THERE ARE SO MANY POSSIBILIES THAT CAN HAPPEN WITH DOTS TOO like "stdio.buffers" and more)
import networking; // looks for this in builtins and imports the whole module which allows to be used like "networking.invoke()"
import networking as net; // this is using the "as" keyword for name changing which is super useful
import "tests/samples/macros.fin"; // this is a normal file import which imports macros as "macros" by default so it can be used like "macros.magic_add!(...)"

import "somelib"; // IMPORTANT: THIS IS SO IMPORTANT BECAUSE WE DO NOT GIVE ANY PATH TO IT SO HOW DOES THE COMPILER KNOW WHERE TO IMPORT THIS LIBRARY, as we assume somelib is a directory containing modules. The compiler also checks an environment variable named "FIN_LIBS" which has multiple paths of imports (specially builtins) and uses those to search for libraries (this is how our package manager "finn" can easily tell our compiler which environment to use using "--fin-libs" flag)

import * from somelib; // this will import ALL symbols from the somelib library

//since namespaces exists we also allow a way to import stuff from namespaces explicitly:
import { HashMap } from hashmap::std; // this means there is a namespace in hashmap library and we explicitly import the `HashMap` symbol from that namespace using COLONCOLON



fun main() <noret> {
    let x <auto> = Vector3{x: 10, y: 10, z: 10};

    printf("Hello i am imported from stdio");

    let y <auto> = macros.magic_add!(10, 20);
  }
